  "query_preview": "..\nToday (Thursday): A cold front will continue traversing eastward \nacross the Inland Northwest through this afternoon and evening, \nbringing breezy winds and leaving dry, cool air in its wake. The \nfront is over the eastern third of WA as of this afternoon and is \nforecast to make it into the ID Panhandle by early this evening. \nHigh temperatures today will be 5-10 degrees cooler than yesterday \nin the mid 80s. Breezy southwest winds will persist into the evening \nwith a tight surface pressure gradient across the region. Expect \nsustained wind speeds of 15-25 mph and gusts of 25-35 mph. Central \nWA, particularly the Okanogan Valley and Waterville Plateau, will \nsee wind speeds and gusts at the high end of those ranges as well as \nminimum relative humidity values dropping into the upper teens. A \nRed Flag Warning has been issued for these areas through 8PM tonight \ndue to critical fire weather conditions resulting from the breezy \nwinds and low relative humidity. Winds will subside overnight as the \nfront moves out of the area. \n\nFriday through Sunday: The Inland Northwest remains under an upper \nlevel trough with shortwave disturbances pivoting around it. Friday \nwill remain dry and a bit breezy. Friday afternoon high temperatures \nwill be 2-5 degrees cooler than today's in the upper 70s to low 80s. \nA deeper and colder system arrives Saturday into Sunday, bringing \nour temperatures down even further with afternoon highs over the \nweekend forecast to peak in the low to mid 70s (upper 70s for the \nwarmer Lewiston-Clarkston valley). In addition to notably cooler \ntemperatures, this system will bring chances for showers and \nthunderstorms to the Cascades, over the higher terrain along the \nCanadian border, and across Southeast WA and the central ID \nPanhandle. While there will be enough mid-level moisture and \ninstability for showers, lower levels of the atmosphere will still \nbe quite dry, so rain amounts will be light with most of the \naforementioned locations seeing less than 0.10 inches of total \nprecipitation. Classic inverted-V model soundings indicate potential \nfor gusty downdraft winds with any showers or storms that develop.\nWinds will also just be breezy in general as Saturday's system moves \nthrough, even where there are no showers or storms. Expect \nwest/southwest winds with gusts of 20-30 mph. Sunday will be less \nwindy and largely dry with chances for precipitation confined to the \neastern ID Panhandle as the weather system shifts east out of the \nregion. \n\nIt's also worth mentioning that low temperatures are forecast to \nfall into the upper 30s and 40s Saturday night into Sunday morning, \npotentially leading to frost development for some of the colder \nsheltered valleys. Those in sheltered valleys may also wake up to \nfog Sunday morning thanks to light winds and residual low level \nmoisture from Saturday's showers.\n\nMonday to Wednesday: A progressive weather pattern continues into \nnext week. A brief return to warm and dry conditions is expected \nMonday and Tuesday, then another system moves in by Wednesday, \nbringing back cooler temperatures and additional chances for \nprecipitation. /Fewkes",
